---
import SubpageLayout from "../layouts/SubpageLayout.astro";
import BuyLinks from "../components/BuyLinks.astro";
import { withBase } from "../utils/url";

const toc = [
  { href: "#overview", label: "Overview" },
  { href: "#pages-in-this-area", label: "Pages in this area" },
];
---

<SubpageLayout
  title="Appendices"
  description="Appendices A through D of The Day After AI: The C-DAD Specification, MCP Documentation and Implementation Resources, The Agent Onboarding Test, and The Interaction Contract Template."
  section="chapters"
  crumbLabel="Appendices"
  toc={toc}
>
  <h1>Appendices</h1>

  <section id="overview">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      <em>The Day After AI</em> closes with four appendices. The site
      glossary is companion vocabulary. It is not one of them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="pages-in-this-area">
    <h2>Pages in this area</h2>
    <ul class="appendix-list">
      <li>
        <a href={withBase("/appendix/c-dad-specification/")}>Appendix A: The C-DAD Specification</a>
        <p>Contract-Driven AI Development, C-DAD, is the framework I set out in a white paper. Its premise is that contracts, not code, should be the surface on which humans, AI agents, and runtimes collaborate.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={withBase("/appendix/mcp-documentation-and-implementation-resources/")}>Appendix B: MCP Documentation and Implementation Resources</a>
        <p>Contracts first, MCP last. This appendix is for teams whose capabilities already have complete contracts; it does not cover standing up an MCP server.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={withBase("/appendix/agent-onboarding-test/")}>Appendix C: The Agent Onboarding Test</a>
        <p>The agent onboarding test checks whether a capability's contract is enough, on its own, for an agent to navigate the capability safely. It evaluates the contract, not the agent.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={withBase("/appendix/interaction-contract-template/")}>Appendix D: The Interaction Contract Template</a>
        <p>In Chapter 10, a component exists either as a picture or as a declaration. This appendix is the working material for the second option.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <BuyLinks />

  <nav class="chapter-nav" aria-label="Chapter navigation">
    <a href={withBase("/company-that-gets-there-first/")}>&larr; Chapter 12: The Company That Gets There First</a>
    <a href={withBase("/about-the-book/")}>Full table of contents &rarr;</a>
  </nav>
</SubpageLayout>

<style>
  .appendix-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }

  .appendix-list a {
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--border);
    text-underline-offset: 0.15em;
  }

  .appendix-list a:hover {
    color: var(--coral);
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
  }

  .appendix-list p {
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }

  .chapter-nav {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .chapter-nav a {
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .chapter-nav a:hover {
    color: var(--coral);
  }
</style>
